			The rvtt Tensix backend
			=======================

This directory implements the Tensix vector (SFPU) extensions to the RISC-V
backend.  This file is a map of it; read it before the sources.

Machine descriptions here are named rvtt-*.md and are ordinary GCC .md files
reached from ../riscv.md -- an rvtt-*.md file is a machine description,
never Markdown.


1. What the passes are for
--------------------------

The Tensix SFPU is a 32-lane vector unit with a small architectural register
file (8 LREGs), a tile-addressed destination file (Dst) reached through
implicit read/write counters (RWC), and two mechanisms for amortising
instruction delivery: replay (record a short sequence once, re-launch it
cheaply) and SFPLOADMACRO (a descriptor-driven macro engine).  Every word the
RISC-V core pushes costs an issue slot, so most of the optimisation effort
is about delivering fewer words, not doing less work.

Design rule: no pass may recognise an operation by name, by a
coefficient fingerprint, or by a magic instruction word.  A transform that
cannot be derived from typed program structure must refuse -- by name, with
a recorded reason (see section 5).


2. Pass families
----------------

  macro tables               SFPLOADMACRO target capability tables and
			     template-slot ownership:
			     rvtt-macro-{tables,ownership}.
  scheduling                 Post-RA scheduling of the SFPU instruction stream
			     around hardware hazards.
  replay formation           Record/playback formation, including hoisting a
			     record out of a loop.
  prgm constants             Constant-register promotion and the priced
			     residency walk that keeps invariants resident.
  effects                    rvtt-effects.h is the single source of truth for
			     instruction effect classification.  New code must
			     query it rather than write another switch.  It
			     also holds the shared builtin-call helpers
			     (argument readers, id predicates); add a new
			     shared helper there rather than in a pass.

Supporting infrastructure that is not a pass: rvtt-cost / rvtt-delivery-cost
/ rvtt-timing / rvtt-trips (pricing engines), rvtt-bnb and rvtt-lpsolve (an
exact branch-and-bound solver used where a heuristic would guess), and the
opt-in host-compiled unit harnesses wired up in t-riscv-tt.


3. Where passes run
-------------------

Registrations live in rvtt-passes.def.  They cluster on a few anchors:
pass_all_optimizations and pass_expand for the GIMPLE passes, and the
passes around register allocation (chiefly pass_postreload) for
the RTL passes.

IMPORTANT: within a single anchor, ordering is encoded by textual position in
rvtt-passes.def, and INSERT_PASS_AFTER reverses it -- each later line on the
same anchor lands closer to the anchor and therefore runs earlier.  This is
the least obvious thing in the directory; check it before assuming an
ordering.


4. Flags: what is on, what is not
---------------------------------

The -mtt- options are declared in ../riscv.opt.  An optimisation flag with
Init(1) is on by default and one with Init(0) is off.  Init(-1) means
unset: the errata workarounds then follow the selected cpu and the numeric
thresholds fall back to their cost-table defaults.  Removed options are kept
as Undocumented records so that using one is diagnosed with an error.

The flags enabled by default are:

	-mtt-tensix-optimize-cc
	-mtt-tensix-optimize-dce
	-mtt-tensix-optimize-replay
	-mtt-tensix-optimize-setexp-fold

HOW TO ENABLE THE REST.  Everything else is opt-in on the command line:

	riscv-tt-elf-g++ -mtt-tensix-optimize-<name> ...

and can be turned back off with the -mno- spelling.  Check what a build is
actually using rather than assuming:

	riscv-tt-elf-g++ -Q --help=target | grep '^ *-mtt-'

A flag being present is not a recommendation to use it.  The default-off
options are a mix of optimisations, experimental knobs and measurement
aids; the testing-only and measurement-only knobs are marked Undocumented.
To see what a pass did or why it declined, add -fdump-<rtl|tree>-<pass name>
and read the refusal (section 5).

A new pass is added disabled by default, and with its flag off the emitted
code is byte-identical to that of the compiler without the pass.


5. Refusals
-----------

rvtt-refusals.def is a registry of named reasons a transform declined.  Every
refusal a pass can emit is registered there; t-riscv-tt regenerates
rvtt-refusals.txt from it and fails the build on a duplicate or unregistered
name.  "It did not fire" is therefore never silent -- there is always a name.

The registry is append-only by rule, and the testsuite pins dump text, so
removing a name can break a test that asserts the exact string.  Note also
that some names are composed at run time (rvtt_refusal_fire_composed), so a
name absent from a grep of the sources is not necessarily dead.


6. Vocabulary
-------------

Terms used throughout the sources that are not standard GCC:

  Dst		 the destination tile register file, addressed in 16x16 faces
  RWC		 read/write counters -- implicit Dst address pointers advanced
		 by SETRWC/INCRWC, which passes must model to know which face
		 is live
  LREG		 one of the 8 architectural SFPU vector registers
  replay	 record a short instruction sequence once, re-launch cheaply
  MOP		 the Tensix zero-overhead hardware loop engine
  row		 one issue of a parameterised instruction sequence
  delivered word the unit the delivery cost model prices
  refusal	 a named, recorded decision not to transform (section 5)
  priced	 a decision taken by asking a cost engine, not a heuristic
  licensed	 an optimisation permitted to change numerical results under
		 an explicit option, backed by a machine-checked divergence
		 certificate
  proof		 an entry under proofs/ backing a transform's legality claim


7. Testing
----------

Tests are in ../../../testsuite/g++.target/riscv/tt/.  Most passes carry
dump-scan tests keyed to their dump name; the original lowering passes are
covered by assembly scans instead.

proofs/ holds machine-checked equivalence results.  Two of them --
store-sink-roundtrip and stochrnd-store-round -- are checked by the build:
t-riscv-tt regenerates rvtt-storefold-verdicts.def from their RESULT files and
fails if the checked-in table is not byte-identical.  The others are cited,
from the sources or from proofs/README.md, as the legality argument for a
transform or for the refusal it justifies, and are not re-checked by the
build.  native-compare-gtle is not cited by any source: it records the
equivalence behind -mtt-tensix-optimize-native-compare, which currently has no
effect (see proofs/README.md).

Host-compiled tooling that is NOT part of the compiler build also lives
here; do not mistake it for dead code:

  *-test.cc		  opt-in unit harnesses, wired up in t-riscv-tt


8. Reading order for a newcomer
-------------------------------

  1. this file
  2. rvtt-passes.def -- the pipeline, with its ordering caveats
  3. rvtt-effects.h -- the effect vocabulary everything else should use
  4. a small self-contained pass, such as gimple-rvtt-int-not.cc, before
     one of the large ones
